  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nTonight through next Thursday...Moisture continues to pool across\nthe region as southerly flow at the surface, ahead of a stalled \ncold front to our west, and aloft persists. Rain chances will \nincrease further Saturday, and again Sunday, when the presence of \na weakening trough will be most observed across the forecast area.\nThis, coupled with added ascent from the sub-tropical jet just to\nour north, daytime heating, and eventually the aforementioned \ncold front pushing through the CWA late Sunday into Monday, will \nbring about the better rain chances across the forecast area this \nweekend.\n\nDespite these high rain chances, the weekend won't be a washout. \nConvection during this timeframe will not be organized.  It'll be \nmore in the form of scattered to numerous showers and storms across \nthe forecast area.  Because of this, the advertised 48-72 hour QPF \nacross the CWA is a little misleading.  While some localized places \ncould indeed see rainfall totals in the 1-3 inch range over the \nweekend, generally for portions of the CWA along and southeast of \nthe Natchez Trace Parkway, these high amounts will be isolated and \nnot widespread across the area as the QPF suggests.  That being \nsaid, in these localized areas that indeed see convection where \nheavy downpours occur in a short amount of time and/or training of \nsuch convection, and potentially observe those higher rainfall \namounts as a result, the ponding of water on roads and in low-lying \nand poor drainage areas will be possible. Severe storms are not \nexpected. However, some gusty winds could accompany some of the more \nintense storms.\n\nRain chances will begin to decrease from northwest to southeast \novernight Sunday into Monday as the cold front shifts southeast \nthrough the forecast area.  Cool dry high pressure will build into \nthe region from the northwest in this front's wake.  These quiet and \ncooler conditions will linger across the CWA through the end of the \nforecast period. /19/",
